<span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:144%;">How do 3-layer attention-only transformers solve this task?</span></span>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_centering ltx_role_start_3_columns"></div><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08184v1/att_1.svg" id="S1.F1.pic3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:434/460;" width="434" height="460"></object>
<span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">L1: extracts single transition probabilities for each lag</span><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08184v1/att_2.svg" id="S1.F1.pic3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:434/460;" width="434" height="460"></object>
<span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">L2: aggregates transition probabilities from the past</span><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.08184v1/att_3.svg" id="S1.F1.pic3.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:434/460;" width="434" height="460"></object>
<span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">L3: <span id="S1.F1.pic3.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">selective induction head</span> select the most likely lag</span>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_centering ltx_role_end_3_columns"></div>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span></span></p>
</span></div>
</span></div></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>. </span><span id="S1.F1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Summary of the framework.<span id="S1.F1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> </span>Top:<span id="S1.F1.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> We define a new task based on Interleaved Markov Chains of different lags (<math id="S1.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> in the example).
</span>Middle:<span id="S1.F1.11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> given a sequence generated from a chain of unknown lag, the model has to identify the true lag, and use it to predict the distribution of the next token.
</span>Bottom:<span id="S1.F1.11.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> attention-only transformers can solve this task with 3 layers. The first computes the transition probabilities for each lag seen during training, the second aggregates these probabilities over the entire past, and finally the third layer implements the selective induction head, which selects the correct lag.
</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Recently, Markov chains have been employed to formulate interesting sequence-to-sequence tasks that can be solved by transformers with interpretable solutions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">ildiz2024from</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nichani2024how</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">makkuva2024attention</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">edelman2024evolution</span>)</cite>.
In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nichani2024how</span></cite> show that transformers trained on Markov Chain sequences learn circuits that capture the causal structure, i.e., the set of parent tokens for each token in the sequence and estimate transition probabilities in-context.
The existing works relying on Markov chains fail to model the nuanced relationships typical of natural language. The same word pair can have different causal relationships depending on the surrounding context. While an effective model should recognize these contextual dependencies, previous research has overlooked this consideration by adopting fixed causal structures.
To address this limitation, we propose <span id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a new synthetic task</span> designed to mimic different causal dependencies (Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4. Markov Chains and Causal Structure Selection ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). We consider <span id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Interleaved Markov Chains</span>, with fixed transition probabilities between states but different underlying causal structures (Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), and theoretically study how 3-layer attention-only transformers learn to correctly predict the next token in a sequence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selective induction heads.</span>
To solve the task at hand—correctly predicting the next token in-context in a sequence generated within this setup (middle of Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>)—transformers need to learn a circuit that adapts to the given context to select the correct causal structure among those seen during training. We call this circuit a <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">selective induction head</span>, as it differs fundamentally from the induction heads introduced so far in the literature, where the circuit learns either to copy a token from a certain position fixed by the unique structure of the data or by comparing its semantics.
In our task, the transformer (with attention maps depicted in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) needs to learn to aggregate all past information to determine from which past position the corresponding token should be copied in order to predict the next token.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A transformer construction for in-context selection.</span>
To understand and formalize the selective heads, we provide an <span id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">interpretable</span> construction of the self-attention layer weights in a 3-layer attention-only disentangled transformer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">friedman2023learning</span>)</cite> that implements this mechanism (Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
We empirically demonstrate that the constructed transformer matches the performance of both disentangled and standard transformers trained from scratch (Sec. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) and that 2-layer attention-only transformers cannot solve the task.
Moreover, we observe that the attention maps of the trained and constructed transformers present the same patterns, further supporting the validity of our algorithm. Finally, we theoretically analyze the predictor implemented by this construction (Sec. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) showing that, in certain cases, it asymptotically converges to the maximum likelihood solution. Our findings provide valuable insights into the mechanisms by which transformers perform model selection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Additional theoretical analyses, omitted proofs (App. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>), extra experiments (Apps. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>), and generalizations of our transformer construction (Apps. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>) are deferred to the appendix.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following the initial empirical observations of the emergent in-context learning capabilities of transformers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">brown2020language</span>)</cite>, several works have attempted to understand this phenomenon. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">xie2021explanation</span></cite> sought to formulate in-context learning as Bayesian inference, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">garg2022can</span></cite> studied the ability of transformers to learn simple functions, such as linear models or multilayer perceptrons, in context.
A subsequent line of work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">akyurek2022learning</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bai2024transformers</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">von2023transformers</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">von2023uncovering</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">raventos2024pretraining</span>)</cite> shows that transformer layers might implement gradient descent to solve in-context linear regression.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">ahn2023transformers</span></cite> extends this idea to higher-order algorithms. Importantly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">olsson2022context</span></cite> postulates that in-context learning is tied to the emergence of induction heads.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bietti2024birth</span></cite>, subsequently extended this idea, showing the development of induction heads to learn bigrams in-context and showcasing a connection with associative memories. More closely related to our work is the literature analyzing transformers through the lens of Markov chains. In particular, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nichani2024how</span></cite> shows how transformers trained on sequences generated by Markov chains on a graph learn simple circuits to capture the underlying causal structure and implement the Bayes-optimal solution by estimating transition probabilities in context.
Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">edelman2024evolution</span></cite> illustrate the formation of statistical induction heads that accurately compute posterior probabilities based on bigram statistics.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">makkuva2024attention</span></cite> used Markov chains to study the loss landscape of transformers, while <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">rajaraman2024transformers</span></cite> show that a constant depth is sufficient to learn k-th order Markov chains.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">svete2024transformers</span></cite> demonstrate that transformers with hard or sparse attention can exactly represent any n-gram model. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">hu2024limitation</span></cite> highlights the limitations of transformers in learning HMMs compared to RNNs.
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nguyen2024understanding</span></cite> recently studied how rules formed out of simple N-gram statistics can approximate transformer predictions; however, the mechanism through which such rules are selected remains unexplained. On the problem of in-context selection, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">bai2023transformers</span></cite> demonstrates that a single transformer can adaptively select between different base algorithms—or even qualitatively different tasks like regression and classification—based on the context provided. Similarly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">yadlowsky2023pretraining</span></cite> studied the ability of transformers to
perform model selection between different function class families.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>(Disentangled) Transformer Models</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the following we introduce the necessary background and notation about the models we use later.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Transformers.</span>
The architecture of decoder-only transformers is built on two fundamental components, the attention mechanism and the multi-layer perceptron (MLP).
Given a finite alphabet <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, transformers map an input sequence <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s=s_{1:T}=(s_{1},\dots,s_{T})\in\mathcal{S}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mi>T</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s=s_{1:T}=(s_{1},\dots,s_{T})\in\mathcal{S}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> to a sequence of vectors <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z=(z_{1},\dots,z_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z=(z_{1},\dots,z_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Each element of the input sequence <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is first encoded using its corresponding one-hot vector, <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{s_{i}}\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{S}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{s_{i}}\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{S}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. These one-hot representations are then mapped to <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional vectors via an embedding matrix <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times|\mathcal{S}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times|\mathcal{S}|}</annotation></semantics></math>. To incorporate positional information, a positional embedding matrix <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> is added. With a slight abuse of notation, let <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th element of the canonical basis of <math id="S3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{T}</annotation></semantics></math> such that each input element <math id="S3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is mapped to a vector <math id="S3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S3.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}=Ee_{s_{i}}+Fe_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}=Ee_{s_{i}}+Fe_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
The information of the different tokens is then mixed by the causal self-attention heads: denoting the key, query and value matrices <math id="S3.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K,Q\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{QK}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K,Q\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{QK}}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and given an input <math id="S3.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>, one gets</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{Attn}(h;Q,K)\coloneq\mathcal{A}(h;Q,K)h^{\top},\quad\textrm{with}\quad\mathcal{A}(h;Q,K)\coloneq\textrm{Softmax}\left(\mathcal{M}(h^{\top}QK^{\top}h);\alpha\right)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>with</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{Attn}(h;Q,K)\coloneq\mathcal{A}(h;Q,K)h^{\top},\quad\textrm{with}\quad\mathcal{A}(h;Q,K)\coloneq\textrm{Softmax}\left(\mathcal{M}(h^{\top}QK^{\top}h);\alpha\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Softmax}(v;\alpha)_{i}\coloneq\frac{\exp{(v_{i}/\alpha)}}{\sum_{j}\exp{(v_{j}/\alpha)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>Softmax</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mstyle><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>α</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Softmax}(v;\alpha)_{i}\coloneq\frac{\exp{(v_{i}/\alpha)}}{\sum_{j}\exp{(v_{j}/\alpha)}}</annotation></semantics></math> is applied row-wise and <math id="S3.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a temperature parameter.
In the following, we call <math id="S3.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attention matrix</span>, <math id="S3.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attention</span>, and <math id="S3.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\textrm{Attn}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\textrm{Attn}:\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S3.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">attention layer</span>.
The causality of the self-attention is enforced by a mask <math id="S3.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, to prevent the model from attending to future tokens i.e.,
<math id="S3.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}(A)_{ij}=A_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}(A)_{ij}=A_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S3.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq j</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-\infty</annotation></semantics></math> otherwise.
For a model with <math id="S3.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> layers and <math id="S3.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{H_{l}\}_{l\in[L]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{H_{l}\}_{l\in[L]}</annotation></semantics></math> attentions heads per layer, we denote by <math id="S3.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q^{(l,h)},K^{(l,h)},V^{(l,h)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q^{(l,h)},K^{(l,h)},V^{(l,h)}</annotation></semantics></math> the attention parameters for the <math id="S3.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th head in the <math id="S3.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>-th layer, <math id="S3.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{1}^{(l)},W_{2}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\textrm{FF}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mtext>FF</mtext></msub></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{1}^{(l)},W_{2}^{(l)}\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times d_{\textrm{FF}}}</annotation></semantics></math> the parameters of the MLP at layer <math id="S3.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>l</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.p2.m35" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{O}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times d}</annotation></semantics></math> the parameters of the output linear layer.
Then, with <math id="S3.p2.m36" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(0)}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(0)}=(x_{1},\ldots,x_{T})\in\mathbb{R}^{d\times T}</annotation></semantics></math> as computed above, the decoder transformer <math id="S3.p2.m37" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{T}(s_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}(s_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math> can be written for <math id="S3.p2.m38" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\dots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\dots,L</annotation></semantics></math>, as</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{h}^{(l)}=h^{(l-1)}+\sum_{h=1}^{H_{l}}\text{Attn}(h^{(l-1)};Q^{(l,h)},K^{(l,h)})V^{(l,h)},\quad h^{(l)}=\tilde{h}^{(l)}+W_{2}^{(l)}\sigma\left(W_{1}^{(l)\top}\tilde{h}^{(l)}\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></munderover><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Q</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>V</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>W</mi><mn>1</mn><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em">⊤</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{h}^{(l)}=h^{(l-1)}+\sum_{h=1}^{H_{l}}\text{Attn}(h^{(l-1)};Q^{(l,h)},K^{(l,h)})V^{(l,h)},\quad h^{(l)}=\tilde{h}^{(l)}+W_{2}^{(l)}\sigma\left(W_{1}^{(l)\top}\tilde{h}^{(l)}\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">where the output is given by <math id="S3.p2.m39" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{O}h^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>O</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{O}h^{(L)}\in\mathbb{R}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Disentangled Transformers.</span>
To improve the interpretability of the operations implemented by the models, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">friedman2023learning</span></cite> propose a transformer architecture in which each layer’s output is concatenated, rather than added, to its input.
This construction makes the residual stream explicitly disentangled, but increases the embedding dimension (constant for standard transformers) with depth.
Additionally, in such <span id="S3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">disentangled transformers</span> the MLP layers are removed, the attention heads are parameterized by a single matrix <math id="S3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}\coloneq QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times d_{\ell}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo>≔</mo><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>K</mi><mo>⊤</mo></msup></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}\coloneq QK^{\top}\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{\ell}\times d_{\ell}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the value matrices are absorbed into the output layer <math id="S3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{W}_{O}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>O</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{W}_{O}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both the token and positional embedding are one-hot encoding, i.e., <math id="S3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> are identity matrices, and we encode the input <math id="S3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math> via concatenation rather than addition.
Altogether, the disentangled transformer <math id="S3.p3.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}(s_{1:T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}(s_{1:T})</annotation></semantics></math> is formalized for <math id="S3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="l=1,\dots,L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">l=1,\dots,L</annotation></semantics></math> as</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\hat{h}^{(l,h)}=\text{Attn}(h^{(l-1)};\tilde{A}^{(l,h)})\quad\text{for}\quad h=1,\dots,H_{l},\quad\text{and}\quad{h}^{(l)}=[h^{(l-1)},\hat{h}^{(l,1)},\ldots,\hat{h}^{(l,H_{l})}]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mtext>Attn</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>for</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>l</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\hat{h}^{(l,h)}=\text{Attn}(h^{(l-1)};\tilde{A}^{(l,h)})\quad\text{for}\quad h=1,\dots,H_{l},\quad\text{and}\quad{h}^{(l)}=[h^{(l-1)},\hat{h}^{(l,1)},\ldots,\hat{h}^{(l,H_{l})}],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the output is <math id="S3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{W}_{O}h^{(L)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>W</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>O</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>L</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{W}_{O}h^{(L)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to the concatenation, the embedding dimension grows over layers as <math id="S3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{l}=(1+H_{l})\cdot d_{l-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>l</mi></msub></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{l}=(1+H_{l})\cdot d_{l-1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{0}=|\mathcal{S}|+T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{0}=|\mathcal{S}|+T</annotation></semantics></math>.
Importantly, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nichani2024how</span></cite> demonstrate that disentangled transformers are equivalent to standard transformers using only attention layers.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Markov Chains and Causal Structure Selection</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address the limitations of existing synthetic settings based on Markov chains and better capture the complexity of natural language, we propose a novel framework. In this framework, the model must learn to select the correct causal structure in-context in order to solve the task and generate the input sequence. In the following, we describe this task in detail and outline its solution.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Interleaved Markov Chains.</span>
The framework consists of sequences of length <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> on a finite alphabet of tokens <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, generated by <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> distinct sources. Let <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{U}=\{U_{1},\dots,U_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{U}=\{U_{1},\dots,U_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of sources and <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\{k_{1},\dots,k_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\{k_{1},\dots,k_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math> a set of positive integers; each source <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> interleaved and identical irreducible aperiodic Markov chains <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">batu2004inferring</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">minot2014separation</span>)</cite>. All the sources are defined by the same transition matrix <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}\in\mathcal{P}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}|}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}\in\mathcal{P}^{|\mathcal{S}|\times|\mathcal{S}|}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of row-stochastic matrices.
This model is equivalent to a time-homogeneous Markov chain <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{(j)}_{t})_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{(j)}_{t})_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> of order <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> , whose transition probabilities depend only on a single state <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> steps back:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=s_{t}\mid X_{t-1}=s_{t-1},\dots,X_{1}=s_{1})=\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=s_{t}\mid X_{t-k_{j}}=s_{t-k_{j}})=s_{t-k_{j}}^{\top}P^{\star}s_{t}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=s_{t}\mid X_{t-1}=s_{t-1},\dots,X_{1}=s_{1})=\mathbb{P}(X_{t}=s_{t}\mid X_{t-k_{j}}=s_{t-k_{j}})=s_{t-k_{j}}^{\top}P^{\star}s_{t}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, we call <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k_{j}\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k_{j}\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> the <span id="S4.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">lag</span> parameter, as defined by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">berchtold2002mixture</span></cite>, where <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}\subseteq\llbracket 1,T\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}\subseteq\llbracket 1,T\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of possible lags. The lag, represented by the edges in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, encodes the causal structure by explicitly representing the causal relationship between the variables in the Markov chain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Data.</span>
Given <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>, a lag is uniformly sampled from <math id="S4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> for each sequence. Denoting the maximum lag by <math id="S4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\max(\mathcal{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\max(\mathcal{K})</annotation></semantics></math>, the first <math id="S4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math> elements of each sequence are sampled from the stationary distribution <math id="S4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P^{\star}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P^{\star}</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring a constant number of independent variables for all sources.
The likelihood of a sequence of lag <math id="S4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{1},\dots,X_{T}\mid k)=\prod_{i=1}^{\hat{k}}\pi(X_{i})\prod_{j=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(X_{j}\mid X_{j-k})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup><mrow><mrow><mi>π</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∏</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{1},\dots,X_{T}\mid k)=\prod_{i=1}^{\hat{k}}\pi(X_{i})\prod_{j=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\mathbb{P}(X_{j}\mid X_{j-k}).</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task.</span>
In this setting, the task is to predict the next state <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{T+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{T+1}</annotation></semantics></math> given an input sequence <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="s_{1:T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>s</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{1:T}</annotation></semantics></math> generated from one of the sources, sampled at random.
However, the identity of the source, and therefore the lag, is unknown.
This task amounts to solving the following minimization problem:</p>
<table id="S4.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f^{\star}=\inf_{f}\,\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}k\sim\text{Unif}[1,\dots,\hat{k}]\\
(X_{1:T})\sim\mathbb{P}(X_{1},\dots,X_{T}\mid k)\end{subarray}}\mathcal{D}_{KL}\left(\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\,\middle\lVert\,f(X_{1},\dots,X_{T})\right)\,," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">=</mo><munder><mo lspace="0.1389em" movablelimits="false" rspace="0.167em">inf</mo><mi>f</mi></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mtable rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mtext>Unif</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>∼</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></msub><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.170em" rspace="0.170em" stretchy="true">∥</mo><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\star}=\inf_{f}\,\mathbb{E}_{\begin{subarray}{c}k\sim\text{Unif}[1,\dots,\hat{k}]\\
(X_{1:T})\sim\mathbb{P}(X_{1},\dots,X_{T}\mid k)\end{subarray}}\mathcal{D}_{KL}\left(\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\,\middle\lVert\,f(X_{1},\dots,X_{T})\right)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{KL}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{KL}</annotation></semantics></math> is the Kullback–Leibler divergence.
Eq. <a href="#S4.E1" title="In 4. Markov Chains and Causal Structure Selection ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></span></a> admits a closed form solution which is the Bayesian model average (BMA), defined as the average of the transition probabilities for each lag, weighted by their posterior probabilities:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{1:T})=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}w_{k}(X_{1:T})\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\;\text{with}\;w_{k}(X_{1:T})=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{1:T}\mid k)\mathbb{P}(k)}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}}\mathbb{P}(X_{1:T}\mid j)\mathbb{P}(j)}\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.280em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext>with</mtext><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo>∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></msub><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{1:T})=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}w_{k}(X_{1:T})\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\;\text{with}\;w_{k}(X_{1:T})=\frac{\mathbb{P}(X_{1:T}\mid k)\mathbb{P}(k)}{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{K}}\mathbb{P}(X_{1:T}\mid j)\mathbb{P}(j)}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.3" class="ltx_p">Asymptotically, the posterior distribution concentrates around the maximum likelihood estimate (MLE) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">rousseau2011asymptotic</span>)</cite>. Let <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> be the lag that maximizes the likelihood for a sequence <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(s_{1},\dots,s_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(s_{1},\dots,s_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,
<math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}=\mathop{\rm arg\,max}_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\,\mathbb{P}(X_{1}=s_{1},\dots,X_{T}=s_{T}\mid k)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>max</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}=\mathop{\rm arg\,max}_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\,\mathbb{P}(X_{1}=s_{1},\dots,X_{T}=s_{T}\mid k)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As <math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\to\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\to\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, the posterior probability <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> converges to 1 for <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k^{*}</annotation></semantics></math> and to 0 for the other lags, i.e., <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="w_{k}\to\mathds{1}[k=k^{*}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{k}\to\mathds{1}[k=k^{*}]</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathds{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>𝟙</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathds{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the indicator function. Then, BMA reduces to selecting the lag with the highest likelihood:</p>
<table id="S4.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{Q}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{1},\dots,X_{T})=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\mathds{1}[k=k^{*}]\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\,." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℚ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></munder><mn>𝟙</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mi>ℙ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Q}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{1},\dots,X_{T})=\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\mathds{1}[k=k^{*}]\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p4.4" class="ltx_p">It is important to note that an interleaved Markov chain of lag <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is mathematically equivalent to a <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>-th order Markov chain with a specific transition structure. Thus, given a set of orders <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> and a sequence generated according to one such order, one could theoretically solve the task by learning in-context the corresponding <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\hat{k}+1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat{k}+1)</annotation></semantics></math>-gram transition probabilities <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">nichani2024how</span>; <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">edelman2024evolution</span>)</cite>. However, such an approach fails to leverage the low-dimensional structure of the problem, resulting in a suboptimal sample complexity of <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{S}|^{\hat{k}+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}(|\mathcal{S}|^{\hat{k}+1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection?</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We now want to understand which algorithm transformers learn during training.
We focus on disentangled transformers as defined in Sec. <a href="#S3" title="3. (Disentangled) Transformer Models ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, which allow for a more interpretable analysis of the model internal computations.
The following proposition, which is the main result of the paper, shows how a disentangled transformer can implement a predictor to solve the in-context selection task.</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmprop1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_prop">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmprop1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 1</span></span><span id="Thmprop1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmprop1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmprop1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmprop1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math> be a contiguous subset of integers, i.e., <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\llbracket\hat{k}-K+1,\hat{k}\rrbracket" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟦</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>−</mo><mi>K</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">⟧</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\llbracket\hat{k}-K+1,\hat{k}\rrbracket</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=|\mathcal{K}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=|\mathcal{K}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}=\max(\mathcal{K})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}=\max(\mathcal{K})</annotation></semantics></math>.
For any <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\geq\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>≥</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\geq\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math>
there exists a three-layer disentangled transformer <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> heads in the second layer such that, defining the normalized transition probabilities <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{i,k}\coloneq\frac{X_{i-k}^{\top}P^{\star}X_{i}}{\sum_{l\in\mathcal{K},l&lt;i}X_{i-l}^{\top}P^{\star}X_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≔</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="false"><msub><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></mrow></msub></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>P</mi><mo>⋆</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{i,k}\coloneq\frac{X_{i-k}^{\top}P^{\star}X_{i}}{\sum_{l\in\mathcal{K},l&lt;i}X_{i-l}^{\top}P^{\star}X_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="Thmprop1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S5.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(3)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\begin{split}\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}(X_{1:T})_{T}=&amp;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\tilde{w}_{k}(X_{1:T})\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\quad\text{with}\quad\tilde{w}_{k}(X_{1:T})=\frac{\exp\left(\frac{\beta}{(T-\hat{k})}\sum_{i=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\tilde{p}_{i,k}\right)}{\sum_{m\in\mathcal{K}}\exp\left(\frac{\beta}{(T-\hat{k})}\sum_{i=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\tilde{p}_{i,m}\right)}\,.\end{split}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" displaystyle="true"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>ℙ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo rspace="1.000em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">with</mtext><mover accent="true"><mi>w</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0.340em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\begin{split}\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}(X_{1:T})_{T}=&amp;\sum_{k\in\mathcal{K}}\tilde{w}_{k}(X_{1:T})\mathbb{P}(X_{T+1}\mid X_{T-k+1})\quad\text{with}\quad\tilde{w}_{k}(X_{1:T})=\frac{\exp\left(\frac{\beta}{(T-\hat{k})}\sum_{i=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\tilde{p}_{i,k}\right)}{\sum_{m\in\mathcal{K}}\exp\left(\frac{\beta}{(T-\hat{k})}\sum_{i=\hat{k}+1}^{T}\tilde{p}_{i,m}\right)}\,.\end{split}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The predictor implemented by the transformer in Eq. <a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a> resembles BMA but differs in how it aggregates past information.
Instead of using the posterior of each model as in BMA, our method employs weights proportional to the exponential of the average of normalized transition probabilities.
We analyze this predictor in Sec. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>, and discuss its convergence to ML.
Proposition <a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates how transformers can implement <span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">selective induction heads</span>, a mechanism that adapts to the input sequence by copying the token correspondent to the lag that maximizes the average normalized transition probabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmprop1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, in Sec. <a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1. Proof of Proposition : Construction for Contiguous Lags ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> below, involves an explicit construction for the weights of the disentangled transformer implementing the solution in Eq. <a href="#S5.E3" title="In Proposition 1. ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></span></a> (an alternative construction for the third layer is in App. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span>).
Notably, this construction produces attention maps similar to those in standard transformers (see Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), suggesting our algorithm closely aligns with trained transformer implementations.
Moreover, we discuss in Sec. <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_label ltx_ref_self"></span> different generalizations, including the construction for non-contiguous lags.
The same construction using a single head implements the same algorithm but results in worse sample complexity. However, in the specific case where <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{K}|=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{K}|=2</annotation></semantics></math>, we provide a different single-head construction that recovers the sample complexity of the multi-head version.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1. </span>Proof of Proposition <a href="#Thmprop1" title="Proposition 1. ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: Construction for Contiguous Lags</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To aid intuition, we use a running example with visual illustrations for <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=10</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{K}=\{1,2,3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{K}=\{1,2,3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall that each input element <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is encoded as <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(0)}_{i}=[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}]\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{S}|+T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(0)}_{i}=[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}]\in\{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{S}|+T}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">First layer: extraction of transition probabilities.</span>
The first attention matrix, <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, consists of two blocks: the first block operates on the semantic component of the input tokens, learning the transpose of the logarithm of the transition matrix<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Here, <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\log P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\log P</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the element-wise logarithm, i.e., <math id="footnote1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\log P)_{ij}=\log(P_{ij})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\log P)_{ij}=\log(P_{ij})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span>. The second block <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> learns the causal relationships induced by each possible lag <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i-k}\to s_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i-k}\to s_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\in\mathcal{K}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒦</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\in\mathcal{K}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td colspan="2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{split}&amp;\widetilde{A}^{(1)}=\begin{pNiceMatrix}\Block[fill=mgold!80,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\log P^{\star\top}&amp;0\\
0&amp;\Block[fill=mlightblue!40,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}A^{(1)}\end{pNiceMatrix}\\
&amp;A^{(1)}_{ij}=\begin{cases}+\lambda\quad\text{if}\quad i-j\in\mathcal{K}\\
-\lambda\quad\text{if}\quad i-j\not\in\mathcal{K}\,.\end{cases}\end{split}\noindent\par\noindent\resizebox{}{}{\scalebox{1}[1]{{\hbox{{\ignorespaces$\qquad A^{(1)}=$
}}}}}\noindent\par\noindent\resizebox{}{}{\scalebox{0.58}[0.58]{{\hbox{{\ignorespaces$\;\;\begin{pNiceMatrix}{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}\\
\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}\\
\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&amp;\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}\\
\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&amp;\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&amp;\Block[fill=mlightblueTrans,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\scalebox{0.8}{+}\lambda&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}&{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}\scalebox{0.8}{-}\lambda}\\
