{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;\Block[fill=orange!35,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\tilde{p}_{9,1}&amp;\Block[fill=red!35,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\tilde{p}_{10,2}\\
{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;\Block[fill=orange!35,rounded-corners]{1-1}{}\tilde{p}_{10,1}\\
{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}&amp;{\color[rgb]{0.55,0.55,0.55}0}\\
\end{pNiceMatrix}$ }}}}}\hskip-2.84526pt</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.7" class="ltx_p">The operation of the first layer is now explicit: for each token <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(0)}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(0)}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, it extracts the normalized transition probabilities <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{i,k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{i,k}</annotation></semantics></math> for each possible lag and stores them in the element <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{i,S+T-k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{i,S+T-k}</annotation></semantics></math>. The resulting vector is subsequently concatenated to the residual stream to be fed to the second layer <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{(1)}_{i}=[[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}],\hat{h}^{(1)}_{i}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{(1)}_{i}=[[e_{s_{i}},e_{i}],\hat{h}^{(1)}_{i}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Second layer: aggregation of transition probabilities.</span>
To predict the next token, the model needs to determine which lag generated the sequence based on the past transitions.
This selection requires aggregating the normalized transition probabilities from the past, and storing them in the embedding of the current token.
However, since consecutive tokens store transition probabilities in overlapping positions, the attention needs to learn a convex combination of tokens that avoid mixing information from different transitions while maximizing the number of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}</annotation></semantics></math> stored (to not discard useful information).
For instance, when aggregating the past for the token at <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=10</annotation></semantics></math> in Eq. <a href="#S5.E4" title="In 5.1. Proof of Proposition : Construction for Contiguous Lags ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></span></a>, summing <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>9</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math> would mix <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{10,2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{10,2}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{p}_{9,1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{p}_{9,1}</annotation></semantics></math> together.
This mixing can be avoided, for example, by only selecting tokens every 3 steps (<math id="S5.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{4},\hat{h}^{(1)}_{7},\hat{h}^{(1)}_{10}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>7</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{4},\hat{h}^{(1)}_{7},\hat{h}^{(1)}_{10}</annotation></semantics></math>) copying transitions without blending information.
More generally, the attention <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{(2,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{(2,1)}</annotation></semantics></math> should attend to every <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>-th token from the current one, which is equivalent to having non-zero entries along the diagonals at positions <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nK" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nK</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\in\mathbb{N}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℕ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\in\mathbb{N}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="nK&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">nK&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>.
This structure can be enforced by constructing the attention matrix <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}^{(2,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}^{(2,1)}</annotation></semantics></math> with a single non-zero block operating on the tokens’ positional encoding, as follows:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
