</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where the first <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math> rows and columns are empty because the first <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>k</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{k}</annotation></semantics></math> elements of the sequence are sampled independently from the stationary distribution and therefore not informative.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This construction resolves the issue of overlapping transitions, but copying only a subset of tokens implies losing information from the excluded <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Introducing additional attention heads <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}^{(2,2)},\ldots,\tilde{A}^{(2,H_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}^{(2,2)},\ldots,\tilde{A}^{(2,H_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> with the same form as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{A}^{(2,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>A</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{A}^{(2,1)}</annotation></semantics></math> above overcomes this limitation.
The resulting attentions <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{(2,2)},\ldots\mathcal{A}^{(2,H_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{(2,2)},\ldots\mathcal{A}^{(2,H_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> still follow a diagonal structure as <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}^{(2,1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}^{(2,1)}</annotation></semantics></math> to avoid overlapping transitions, but they are shifted to copy different tokens.
For the given example, we can design <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(2,2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(2,2)}</annotation></semantics></math> as in Eq. <a href="#S5.E5" title="In 5.1. Proof of Proposition : Construction for Contiguous Lags ‣ 5. How Can Transformer Do In-Context Selection? ‣ Selective induction Heads: How Transformers Select Causal Structures in Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_nolink"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></span></a> to attend <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{9}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>9</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{9}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>6</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{6}</annotation></semantics></math>, and similarly, construct <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A^{(2,3)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A^{(2,3)}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{8}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>8</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{8}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{h}^{(1)}_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{h}^{(1)}_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> when computing the output at <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
<table id="S5.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(5)</span></td>
